At SEWA Cooperative Federation, I led strategy, research, and partnerships for climate adaptation initiatives supported by a key grant. Alongside a broader portfolio of projects, I designed and implemented a research initiative focused on generating evidence on the climate risks faced by women-led cooperative businesses.

The project lifecycle spanned conceptualisation, research design, partnership development, community engagement, and project and donor management. 
The project involved three key layers of complexity:

* Developing a climate risk framework for small women's businesses and ensuring direct outcomes for women's enterprise management in an under-researched domain and geography.
* Implementing research with women workers in the informal sector, who were navigating multiple personal and livelihood-related barriers, with limited capacity to engage in research. This required designing a research programme around the women's realities, capacities, and feasibility for participating in research-led initiatives.
* Bridging the gap between multiple stakeholders involved—project implementation partners who were unfamiliar with this niche area of research, international donors to whom we had to clearly communicate our work and challenges, and organisational leadership, who were unfamiliar with the research methods and needed to see tangible outcomes.

Ensuring the research methodology was contextual and well-designed was the foundational piece of the journey, as it would determine success at every subsequent stage of the project. I deliberately planned for an additional buffer during the research design phase, incorporating preliminary engagements and pilot activities to ensure our methods, expected outputs, and intended outcomes aligned with both objectives: (a) supporting enterprise resilience and development, and (b) delivering the outcomes committed to the donor.

The implementation stage with the women's cooperative workers was far from straightforward. We were working with women balancing work, caregiving, and healthcare burdens, with very limited bandwidth to engage with researchers, however minimal the time commitment. This stage required me to be an active listener—to experienced leaders within the movement—and reinforced what it truly means to build solutions with empathy. I evolved the methodology around the women's work schedules, ensuring meaningful immersion. An agile project plan allowed us to continuously adapt our approach without extending timelines, while ensuring the women became partners in the research rather than simply research subjects.

Finally, I led with clear and intentional communication across stakeholders. I worked closely with programme partners to orient them to the nature of the work, consistently linked our activities back to the broader climate adaptation goals of the fund, and consciously rooted my communication in the language and lived experiences of the women workers, ensuring the real purpose of the project was never lost in impact jargon.
